    "q-277045": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277045",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=6-4\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=6-4\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=6-4\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6-4\\sqrt{y} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{y} & =6-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{4}(6-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}(6-x))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\le 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
