    "q-277064": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277064",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{x}}+1.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{x}}+1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}}+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5\\sqrt{y}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{2}{5(x-1)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{5(x-1)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{25(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
