      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{x}}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{y}} & =2-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{6(2-x)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{6(2-x)})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$",
