    "q-148747": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148747",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{4(x-2)} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
