    "q-148501": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148501",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2\\sqrt[3]{3x-1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt[3]{3y-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt[3]{3y-1} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3y-1} & =\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{3y-1})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}x)}^{3} \\\\ 3y-1 & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3} \\\\ 3y & =\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{3}+1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{3}+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{24}({x}^{3}+8).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
