    "q-148494": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148494",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x-1}+3.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x-1}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1}+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{y-1} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{y-1} & =2(x-3) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y-1})}^{2} & ={(2(x-3))}^{2} \\\\ y-1 & =4(x-3)^{2} \\\\ y & =4(x-3)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=4(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1,\\,f(x)\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 3,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-148494-a",
