    "q-148550": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148550",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ x-2 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ 2-x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{3y+2} & =\\frac{1}{2-x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3y+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2-x})}^{2} \\\\ 3y+2 & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 3y & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-\\frac{2}{3},\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
