    "q-277959": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277959",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2,\\,x\\ne \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{81(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{6y-1} & =\\frac{2}{k(x-2)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{6y-1})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{k(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ 6y-1 & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 6y & =\\frac{8}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{81}\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
