    "q-277840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277840",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=16(x-6)^{2}+5.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5} & =x-6 \\\\ \\sqrt{y-5} & =k(x-6) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y-5})}^{2} & ={(k(x-6))}^{2} \\\\ y-5 & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2} \\\\ y & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 5,\\,f(x)\\ge 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 5.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
