    "q-148570": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148570",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "The function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x+4)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3y+1} & =x-k \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{3y+1})}^{3} & ={(x-k)}^{3} \\\\ 3y+1 & =(x-k)^{3} \\\\ 3y & =(x-k)^{3}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k=4\\,⟹\\,k=-4.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
